"Web Animations Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/)\nciteturn1273view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/\",\"lineno\":1758}); Total lines: 4503\nL1747: The offset of a keyframe is a value in the range [0, 1] or the special value null. The list of cite379†keyframes for a cite237†keyframe effect must be loosely sorted by offset, which means that for each keyframe in the list that has a cite380†keyframe offset that is not null, the offset must be greater than or equal to the offset of the previous keyframe in the list with a keyframe offset that is not null, if any.\nL1748: The behavior when cite379†keyframes overlap or have unsupported values is defined in cite103†§ 5.3.4 The effect value of a keyframe effect .\nL1749: \nL1750: Each keyframe also has a keyframe-specific easing function associated with it, which is an cite336†easing function that is applied to the period of time between the keyframe on which it is specified and the next keyframe in the list.\nL1751: \nL1752: Note: The cite336†easing function specified on the last keyframe in the list is never applied.\nL1753: Each cite379†keyframe may have a keyframe-specific composite operation that, if set, is applied to all values specified in that keyframe. The possible operations and their meanings are identical to those defined for the cite381†composite operation associated with the cite237†keyframe effect as a whole in cite108†§ 5.4.4 Effect composition .\nL1754: If the cite382†keyframe-specific composite operation for a keyframe is not set, the composite operation specified for the keyframe effect as a whole is used for values specified in that keyframe.\nL1755: Tests\nL1756: \nL1757:   * cite383†applying-interpolated-transform.html†wpt.fyi cite384†(live test)†wpt.live cite385†(source)†github.com L1758: #### 5.3.2. Computing property values\nL1759: \nL1760: To compute a property value given a property `property`, a value `value`, and an `cite373†Element†dom.spec.whatwg.org ` `element`: resolve `value` according to the “Computed Value” line of the `property`’s definition table, using the cite362†computed values of `element` as the context for resolving dependencies, and return the result.\nL1761: \nL1762: Note: The cite362†computed values on `element` are not affected by this algorithm.\nL1763: This algorithm implies that property values specified in keyframes can establish order dependencies. When cite386†computing a property value , the cite362†computed values of dependencies held by `value` must be calculated first.\nL1764: \nL1765:     var animation = elem.animate([{ fontSize: '10px', width: '10em' },\nL1766:                                   { fontSize: '20px', width: '20em' }], 1000);\nL1767:     animation.currentTime = 500;\nL1768:     console.log(getComputedStyle(elem).fontSize); // Should be 15px\nL1769:     console.log(getComputedStyle(elem).width); // Should be 225px\nL1770: In this example, in order to cite386†compute a property value for `10em`, we need to know the cite362†computed value of cite387†font-size on the cite374†target element , which in turn is determined by the cite378†effect value for font-size, which in turn depends on computing property values for font-size. Hence, computing property values are subject to ordering constraints.\nL1771: #### 5.3.3. Calculating computed keyframes\nL1772: \nL1773: Before calculating the cite378†effect value of a cite237†keyframe effect , the property values on its cite379†keyframes are cite101†computed , and the offset to use for any keyframes with a null cite380†keyframe offset is computed. The result of resolving these values is a set of computed keyframes.\nL1774: \nL1775: The calculated cite380†keyframe offsets of a set of cite379†keyframe that includes suitable values for each null keyframe offset are referred to as the computed keyframe offsets.\nL1776: To produce cite388†computed keyframe offsets , we define a procedure to compute missing keyframe offsets that takes a sequence of cite379†keyframes , `keyframes`, and has the following steps:\nL1777: \nL1778:   1. For each cite379†keyframe in `keyframes`, let the cite388†computed keyframe offset of the keyframe be equal to its cite380†keyframe offset value.\nL1779:   2. If `keyframes` contains more than one cite379†keyframe and the cite388†computed keyframe offset of the first keyframe in `keyframes` is null, set the computed keyframe offset of the first keyframe to 0.\nL1780: \nL1781:   3. If the cite388†computed keyframe offset of the last cite379†keyframe in `keyframes` is null, set its computed keyframe offset to 1.\nL1782: \nL1783:   4. For each pair of cite379†keyframes `A` and `B` where:\nL1784: \nL1785:      * `A` appears before `B` in `keyframes`, and\nL1786:      * `A` and `B` have a cite388†computed keyframe offset that is not null, and\nL1787: \nL1788:      * all cite379†keyframes between `A` and `B` have a null cite388†computed keyframe offset ,\nL1789: \nL1790: calculate the cite388†computed keyframe offset of each cite379†keyframe between `A` and `B` as follows:\nL1791: \nL1792:     1. Let offset_{`k`} be the cite388†computed keyframe offset of a cite379†keyframe `k`.\nL1793: \nL1794:     2. Let `n` be the number of keyframes between and including `A` and `B` minus 1.\nL1795:     3. Let `index` refer to the position of `keyframe` in the sequence of keyframes between `A` and `B` such that the first keyframe after `A` has an `index` of 1.\nL1796: \nL1797:     4. Set the cite388†computed keyframe offset of `keyframe` to cite389†offset _{`A`} + (offset_{`B`} − offset_{`A`}) × `index` / `n`.\nL1798: \nL1799: Tests\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn1273view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/\",\"lineno\":1997}); Total lines: 2842\nL1986: cite132†Arbitrary substitution functions are cite177†substituted during style cite179†computation , before any other value transformations or introspection can occur. If a property, after cite537†property replacement , does not match its declared grammar, the declaration is cite492†invalid at computed-value time .\nL1987: Note: Since cite132†arbitrary substitution functions resolve at cite179†computed value time, if the resulting value after substitution is invalid, the property falls back (essentially) to cite1090†unset behavior, rather than falling back to an earlier value in the cite488†cascade the way declarations invalid at parse time do. See cite85†Invalid Substitution .\nL1988: \nL1989: Tests\nL1990:   * cite1091†variable-declaration-16.html†wpt.fyi cite1092†(live test)†wpt.live cite1093†(source)†github.com L1991:   * cite1094†variable-declaration-17.html†wpt.fyi cite1095†(live test)†wpt.live cite1096†(source)†github.com L1992:   * cite1097†variable-declaration-18.html†wpt.fyi cite1098†(live test)†wpt.live cite1099†(source)†github.com L1993:   * cite1100†variable-declaration-19.html†wpt.fyi cite1101†(live test)†wpt.live cite1102†(source)†github.com L1994:   * cite1103†variable-declaration-21.html†wpt.fyi cite1104†(live test)†wpt.live cite1105†(source)†github.com L1995:   * cite1106†variable-transitions-transition-property-all-before-value.html†wpt.fyi cite1107†(live test)†wpt.live cite1108†(source)†github.com L1996:   * cite1109†variable-transitions-value-before-transition-property-all.html†wpt.fyi cite1110†(live test)†wpt.live cite1111†(source)†github.com L1997: If a property value, after cite537†property replacement , contains only a single cite490†CSS-wide keyword (and possibly whitespace/comments), its value is determined as if that keyword were its cite399†specified value all along.\nL1998: \nL1999: Tests\nL2000:   * cite1112†whitespace-in-fallback-crash.html†wpt.fyi cite1113†(live test)†wpt.live cite1114†(source)†github.com L2001:   * cite1115†wide-keyword-fallback-001.html†wpt.fyi cite1116†(live test)†wpt.live cite1117†(source)†github.com L2002:   * cite1118†wide-keyword-fallback-002.html†wpt.fyi cite1119†(live test)†wpt.live cite1120†(source)†github.com L2003: For example, the following usage is fine from a syntax standpoint, but results in nonsense when the variable is substituted in:\nL2004: \nL2005:     :root { --looks-valid: 20px; }\nL2006:     p { background-color: var(--looks-valid); }\nL2007: \nL2008: \nL2009: Since 20px is an invalid value for cite1121†background-color , the property becomes cite492†invalid at computed-value time , and instead resolves to cite1122†transparent (the cite1123†initial value for background-color).\nL2010: If the property was one that’s inherited by default, such as cite1124†color , it would compute to the inherited value rather than the initial value.\nL2011: \nL2012: While a cite133†var() function can’t get a cite490†CSS-wide keyword from the cite548†custom property itself—​if you tried to specify that, like --foo: initial;, it would just trigger cite1125†explicit defaulting for the custom property—​it can have a CSS-wide keyword in its fallback:\nL2013: \nL2014:     p { color: var(--does-not-exist, initial); }\nL2015: In the above code, if the --does-not-exist property didn’t exist or is cite492†invalid at computed-value time , the cite133†var() will instead substitute in the cite1126†initial keyword, making the property behave as if it was originally cite1124†color: initial . This will make it take on the document’s initial color value, rather than defaulting to inheritance, as it would if there were no fallback.\nL2016: \nL2017: To replace substitution functions in a property `prop`:\nL2018:   1. cite177†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions in `prop`’s value, given «\"property\", `prop`’s name» as the cite535†substitution context . Let `result` be the returned cite174†component value sequence.\nL2019: \nL2020:   2. If `result` contains the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value , `prop` is cite492†invalid at computed-value time ; return.\nL2021: \nL2022:   3. cite166†Parse `result` according to `prop`’s grammar. If this returns failure, `prop` is cite492†invalid at computed-value time ; return.\nL2023:   4. Otherwise, replace `prop`’s value with the parsed result.\nL2024: \nL2025: Tests\nL2026:   * cite1127†css-variable-change-style-001.html†wpt.fyi cite1128†(live test)†wpt.live cite1129†(source)†github.com L2027:   * cite1130†css-variable-change-style-002.html†wpt.fyi cite1131†(live test)†wpt.live cite1132†(source)†github.com L2028:   * cite1133†variable-declaration-01.html†wpt.fyi cite1134†(live test)†wpt.live cite1135†(source)†github.com L2029:   * cite1136†variable-declaration-02.html†wpt.fyi cite1137†(live test)†wpt.live cite1138†(source)†github.com L2030:   * cite1139†variable-declaration-03.html†wpt.fyi cite1140†(live test)†wpt.live cite1141†(source)†github.com L2031:   * cite1142†variable-declaration-04.html†wpt.fyi cite1143†(live test)†wpt.live cite1144†(source)†github.com L2032:   * cite1145†variable-declaration-05.html†wpt.fyi cite1146†(live test)†wpt.live cite1147†(source)†github.com L2033:   * cite1148†variable-generated-content-dynamic-001.html†wpt.fyi cite1149†(live test)†wpt.live cite1150†(source)†github.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Properties and Values API Level 1 (https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/)\nciteturn1273view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/\",\"lineno\":196}); Total lines: 1339\nL167: For example, suppose --url-foo and --url-bar are registered custom properties with <url> syntax, and that we have a stylesheet at `/style/foo/foo.css`:\nL168: \nL169:     div {\nL170:       --url-foo: url(\"foo.png\");\nL171:     }\nL172: \nL173: \nL174: and another stylesheet at `/style/bar/bar.css`\nL175: \nL176:     div {\nL177:       --url-bar: url(\"bar.png\");\nL178:     }\nL179: and finally a document at `/index.html`:\nL180: \nL181:     <link href=\"/style/foo/foo.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\">\nL182:     <link href=\"/style/bar/bar.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\">\nL183:     <div style=\"background-image: var(--url-foo), var(--url-bar);\">\nL184:     </div>\nL185: \nL186: \nL187: Here, the var(--url-foo) reference would produce a URL that resolves against `/style/foo`, and the var(--url-bar) reference would produce a URL that resolves against `/style/bar`.\nL188: On the other hand, if both --url-foo and --url-bar were unregistered, they would substitute their literal values (relative URLs) into the `/index.html` stylesheet, which would then resolve the URLs against `/index.html` instead.\nL189: \nL190: For `\"<image>\"` values, the computed value is the cite104†computed <image>†drafts.csswg.org .\nL191: \nL192: For `\"<transform-function>\"` and `\"<transform-list>\"` values, the computed value is as specified but with all lengths resolved to their computed values.\nL193: For values with cite40†multipliers , the computed value is a list of the computed values of the base type.\nL194: \nL195: For syntaxes specified with cite41†the | combinator , the computed value is given by applying the computed-value rules for the first clause that matches the value.\nL196: ### 2.5. Animation Behavior\nL197: \nL198: Note: As defined by cite105†[css3-animations] and cite106†[css3-transitions] , it is possible to specify animations and transitions that reference custom properties.\nL199: \nL200: When referenced by animations and transitions, custom property values cite107†interpolate†drafts.csswg.org cite108†by computed value†drafts.csswg.org , in accordance with the type that they parsed as.\nL201: Note: This implies that a list of values, such as `<color>+` or `<color>#`, will interpolate as a simple list, matching up each component index-by-index, and failing if the number of components doesn’t match.\nL202: \nL203: As an exception to the above rule, a value that parsed as a `<transform-list>`, a `<transform-function>`, or a `<transform-function>+` instead interpolates as per the cite109†transform†drafts.csswg.org property.\nL204: Note: If, for whatever reason, a custom property is defined with a syntax of `<transform-function>#`, this will thus first interpolate as a simple list, and then each list item will interpolate as a cite109†transform†drafts.csswg.org value.\nL205: \nL206: Note: Registering (or changing the registration) of a custom property can change its computed value, which can start or interrupt a CSS transition.\nL207: ### 2.6. Conditional Rules\nL208: \nL209: As stated in cite23†§ 2.2 Parse-Time Behavior , both unregistered and cite81†registered cite77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org accept (almost) all possible values at parse-time. cite81†Registered cite77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org only apply their syntax at cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org time.\nL210: So, all cite77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org , regardless of whether they’re cite81†registered or unregistered, will test as \"true\" in an cite110†@supports†drafts.csswg.org rule, so long as you don’t violate the (very liberal) generic syntax for cite77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org .\nL211: For example, even if a custom property is registered with `syntax: \"<color>\";`, a rule like `@supports (--foo: 1em) {...}` will still evaluate as true and apply those styles, because the declaration does successfully parse as a valid property.\nL212: ### 2.7. Substitution via cite89†var()†drafts.csswg.org L213: \nL214: Like unregistered custom properties, the value of a registered custom property can be substituted into another value with the cite89†var()†drafts.csswg.org function. However, registered custom properties substitute as their cite25†computed value , rather than the original token sequence used to produce that value.\nL215: Any cite89†var()†drafts.csswg.org function that references a registered custom property must be replaced with an equivalent token sequence, which is equal to the token sequence that would have been produced by cite111†serializing†drafts.csswg.org the computed value, and cite112†tokenizing†drafts.csswg.org the resulting string.\nL216: Suppose that --x is registered with <length> syntax, and that --y is an unregistered custom property.\nL217: \nL218:     div {\nL219:       font-size: 10px;\nL220:       --x: 8em;\nL221:       --y: var(--x);\nL222:     }\nL223: \nL224: \nL225: Because the computed value of --x (when serialized) is \"80px\", the computed value of --y is a cite113†<dimension-token>†drafts.csswg.org with a value of \"80\" and unit \"px\".\nL226: #### 2.7.1. Dependency Cycles via Relative Units\nL227: \nL228: cite81†Registered custom properties follow the same rules for dependency cycle resolution as unregistered cite77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org , with the following additional constraints:\nL229: \nL230: For any registered custom property with a cite114†<length>†drafts.csswg.org or cite115†<length-percentage>†drafts.csswg.org syntax component:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[css-animations] Resolving dependencies in keyframes · Issue #5125 · w3c/csswg-drafts · GitHub (https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/5125)\nciteturn1273view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/5125\",\"lineno\":145}); Total lines: 271\nL84:     * PROGRAMS\nL85:       * cite50†Security Lab†securitylab.github.com L86:       * cite51†Maintainer Community†maintainers.github.com L87:       * cite52†Accelerator L88:       * cite53†GitHub Stars†stars.github.com L89:       * cite54†Archive Program†archiveprogram.github.com L90: \nL91:     * REPOSITORIES\nL92:       * cite55†Topics L93:       * cite56†Trending L94:       * cite57†Collections L95: \nL96:   * Enterprise\nL97: \nL98:     * ENTERPRISE SOLUTIONS\nL99:       * cite19†Enterprise platformAI-powered developer platform L100:     * AVAILABLE ADD-ONS\nL101:       * cite10†GitHub Advanced SecurityEnterprise-grade security features L102:       * cite58†Copilot for BusinessEnterprise-grade AI features L103:       * cite59†Premium SupportEnterprise-grade 24/7 support L104: \nL105:   * cite60†Pricing L106: \nL107: Search`/`\nL108: \nL109: cite1†Sign in L110: \nL111: cite61†Sign up L112: \nL113: Appearance settings\nL114: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL115: ### Uh oh!\nL116: \nL117: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL118: \nL119: cite62†w3c / cite63†csswg-drafts Public\nL120: \nL121:   * cite64†Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\nL122:   * cite64†Fork 807 L123:   * cite64†Star L124: \nL125:   * cite63†Code L126:   * cite65†Issues 4.5k L127:   * cite66†Pull requests 125 L128:   * cite67†Discussions L129:   * cite68†Actions L130:   * cite69†Projects L131:   * cite70†Security and quality 0 L132:   * cite71†Insights L133: \nL134: Additional navigation options\nL135: # [css-animations] Resolving dependencies in keyframes #5125\nL136: \nL137: New issue\nL138: \nL139: Copy link\nL140: \nL141: New issue\nL142: \nL143: Copy link\nL144: \nL145: Closed\nL146: \nL147: cite72†#5255 L148: \nL149: Closed\nL150: \nL151: cite73†[css-animations] Resolving dependencies in keyframes #5125\nL152: \nL153: cite72†#5255 L154: \nL155: Copy link\nL156: \nL157: Labels\nL158: \nL159: cite74†css-animations-1Current Work Current Work\nL160: ## Description\nL161: \nL162: cite75†andruud L163: \nL164: opened cite76†on May 28, 2020 L165: \nL166: Issue body actions\nL167: \nL168: This came up (again) during cite77†@kevers-google 's in-progress implementation of getKeyframes in Chrome. Since getKeyframes directly exposes computed keyframes, it forces us to deal with how/when dependencies in the keyframe are resolved in more detail.\nL169: We should in my opinion take a \"broad\" approach of thinking about all dependencies, and not specify detailed behavior per case. For example, `1em` has a dependency on `font-size`, and `var(--x)` has a dependency on `--x`. In terms of dependencies, `1em` and `var(--x)` are very similar, so for consistency, these cases should be treated the same way.\nL170: Browsers generally don't agree on how interpolation actually happens when complicated dependencies are involved. I won't enumerate all differences, but instead highlight this (well-known) example:\nL171: \nL172:     `@keyframes test {\nL173:       from { font-size: 2px; width: 10em; }\nL174:       to { font-size: 4px; width: 20em; }\nL175:     }\nL176: \nL177:     div {\nL178:       font-size: 10px;\nL179:       animation: test 10s -5s linear paused; /* t=0.5 */\nL180:     }\nL181:     `\nL182: \nL183: The computed value of `width` on `div` for the different browsers:\nL184:   * Firefox: `150px`. (1)\nL185:   * Safari: `50px`. (2)\nL186:   * Chrome: `45px`. (3)\nL187: \nL188: cite77†@kevers-google , cite78†@flackr and myself have had several discussion about this recently, and three different ways of dealing with it surfaced in those discussions:\nL189: ## 1. Resolving against the pre-animated style\nL190: \nL191: In this model, the `em` units would resolve against the would-be computed value of `font-size` without animation effects, i.e. `10px` in the first example. The interpolation for `width` would take place in absolute values, between `100px` and `200px`. For `var()` references, they would behave similarly: the value would be substituted using the pre-animated computed value of the referenced custom property.\nL192: I think this solution is a nice one in terms of technical complexity, but I'm not sure if it matches author expectation.\nL193: \nL194: This appears to be what Firefox is doing (for `em` units).\nL195: ## 2. Resolving against local values in the keyframe\nL196: \nL197: In this approach, each keyframe is basically a list of declarations that's added to the cascade, and we derive computed values from that. In approximated spec language:\nL198:   * Let base cascade be the cascade as it would be without animation/transition affects added.\nL199:   * For each specified keyframe:\nL200:     * Let keyframe cascade be a copy of the base cascade\nL201:     * Add the declarations of the keyframe to the keyframe cascade, at the animations level.\nL202:     * Produce a computed keyframe by substituting each value in the specified keyframe with the corresponding computed value from the keyframe cascade.\nL203:   * Interpolation then happens between computed keyframes.\nL204: In the first example, `em` units would then resolve against the `font-size` specified locally in the keyframe (if present). The interpolation of `width` would be between `20px` and `80px`.\nL205: \nL206: This approach would match my initial expectation as an author, I think. But not sure how practical it would be to implement this. Although Safari has the apparent behavior of this model already, at least for the example I gave.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[CSSWG] Minutes Lisbon F2F 2016-09-19 Part V: Transforms, Variables, Transitions/Animations, Cascade, CR Plan, January Meeting [css-transforms] [css-variables] [css-transitions] [css-animations] [css-cascade] from Dael Jackson on 2016-11-16 (www-style@w3.org from November 2016) (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/www-style/2016Nov/0073.html)\nciteturn1273view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/www-style/2016Nov/0073.html\",\"lineno\":27}); Total lines: 725\nL0: # [CSSWG] Minutes Lisbon F2F 2016-09-19 Part V: Transforms, Variables, Transitions/Animations, Cascade, CR Plan, January Meeting [css-transforms] [css-variables] [css-transitions] [css-animations] [css-cascade]\nL1: \nL2:   * This message:\nL3:     * cite0†Message body L4:     * Respond\nL5:     * cite1†More options L6:   * Related messages:\nL7:     * cite2†Next message L8:     * cite3†Previous message L9:   * From: Dael Jackson <daelcss@gmail.com>\nL10:   * Date: Tue, 15 Nov 2016 20:58:04 -0500\nL11:   * To: www-style@w3.org\nL12:   * Message-ID: <CADhPm3ttWWe9h_5kxc3CC6jZtFMmw6gLAWcFLwJrpCTmAQFM2w@mail.gmail.com>\nL13: \nL14:     =========================================\nL15:       These are the official CSSWG minutes.\nL16:       Unless you're correcting the minutes,\nL17:      Please respond by starting a new thread\nL18:        with an appropriate subject line.\nL19:     =========================================\nL20: \nL21: \nL22:     Transforms\nL23:     ----------\nL24:       - RESOLVED: Punt 3d transforms to level 2, turn it into a real\nL25:                   spec, quickly REC Transforms 1.\nL26: \nL27:     Variables\nL28:     ---------\nL29: \nL30:       - RESOLVED: Custom properties must preserve their input exactly\nL31:                   for serialization.\nL32:       - RESOLVED: var() function works in keyframes, pulls its value\nL33:                   from the animated value of the custom property on the\nL34:                   element.\nL35: \nL36:     Transitions/Animations\nL37:     ----------------------\nL38:       - RESOLVED: Move the four transition/animation new events to the\nL39:                   level 1 specs.\nL40:       - RESOLVED: After edits, publish new WDS of transitions and\nL41:                   animations?\nL42: \nL43:     Cascade\nL44:     -------\nL45: \nL46:       - RESOLVED: republish Cascade 4 as CR, with the additional note\nL47:                   that inheritance isn't affected by \"revert\".\nL48: \nL49:     CR Plan\nL50:     -------\nL51: \nL52:       - Grid, Selectors, Box Alignment, Media Queries 4 should be in CR\nL53:           before the publication freeze at the end of the year.\nL54:           - Any open items for them will be prioritized on tomorrow's\nL55:               agenda.\nL56:       - RESOLVED: Publish Tables as FPWD.\nL57: \nL58:     January Meeting\nL59:     ---------------\nL60: \nL61:       - Seattle F2F for Jan 11-13 is confirmed, just waiting to see if\nL62:           it's in Seattle proper or in Redmond.\nL63:       - Looking further, Japan is provisionally the week of 17 April,\nL64:           but may not be held if it's decided the group could just have\nL65:           three F2Fs next week (Seattle, summer, TPAC).\nL66: \nL67:     ===== FULL MINUTES BELOW =======\nL68: \nL69:     Agenda: cite4†https://wiki.csswg.org/planning/tpac-2016#agenda†wiki.csswg.org L70: \nL71:     Scribe: TabAtkins\nL72: \nL73:     Transforms\nL74:     ==========\nL75: \nL76:       shane: translate/rotate/scale are in a L2 delta spec.\nL77:       shane: We did that because Transforms 1 was gonna be stable Real\nL78:              Soon Now©\nL79:       shane: So asking if we can move them to L1.\nL80:       * fantasai suggests someone just finish Transforms 1\nL81: \nL82:       dbaron: How many implementations?\nL83:       shane: We have one behind a flag.\nL84:       dbaron: We had someone working on it, but it was harder than I\nL85:               thought.\nL86:       dbaron: To do animation, it needs to be plumbed all the way thru\nL87:               the compositor. Seemed like too much effort.\nL88:       shane: I think our implementation doesn't plumb; it pulls\nL89:              animations back to the main thread if you animate one of\nL90:              the properties.\nL91:       shane: Not a long-term plan, but there's a growing list of\nL92:              properties that need the same treatment. offset-* stuff too.\nL93:       shane: So our intention was to do everything in one blast.\nL94:       shane: Pulling things to compositor isn't particularly expensive\nL95:              anymore.\nL96:       dbaron: I was concerned about the cost of new code needing to be\nL97:               written, not performance.\nL98:       shane: I'd just say that an increased gamut of compositable\nL99:              properties is coming anyway. We're deferring that cost as\nL100:              well, but we'll have to go back and pay it too.\nL101: \nL102:       dino: So question is whether to put them into Transforms 1.\nL103:       dino: What's wrong with keeping it in level 2?\nL104:       TabAtkins: CSSWG policy is to not ship publicly until it's stable;\nL105:                  delta specs aren't.\nL106:       dino: Problem remaining is some 3d issues, multiple\nL107:             implementations that all disagree a bit.\n"
